---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro';
import UpgradeDiagram from '../../components/UpgradeDiagram.astro';
import DaemonCopyDiagram from '../../components/DaemonCopyDiagram.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Upgrade from 1.x — Tether CLI" description="A checklist to upgrade your machines from Tether 1.x to 2.0 one at a time: sync, trust your machines, and review the Inbox.">
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">GET STARTED</p>
    <h1>Upgrade from 1.x</h1>
    <p>Tether 2.0 signs machine records, checks synced packages before it installs them, and gives packages to profiles. After the upgrade, each machine must trust your other machines once. Until then, packages from those machines wait in the Inbox.</p>
    <UpgradeDiagram />
    <p>Work through this checklist on each machine, one machine at a time.</p>

    <h2 id="before">Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You can upgrade one machine at a time. A sync repo can hold 1.x and 2.0 machines together. Machines on 1.11.10, 1.12.0, 1.13.1 and 1.13.2 read the files that 2.0 writes</li>
      <li>Patch each machine that stays on 1.x for a while to 1.13.2 first. Earlier 1.x builds push their old copy of <code>config.toml</code> back after they apply a synced one. That reverts config changes from other machines. See <a href="#machines-on-1x">Machines on 1.x</a></li>
      <li>Plan to make config changes, such as profiles, after the upgrade. See <a href="/docs/config-merge">Config merge</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="checklist">Checklist for each machine</h2>

    <h3>1. Upgrade the binary</h3>
    <Code code="brew upgrade tether-cli" />
    <p>If you installed a release download or built from source, install the new version the same way. See <a href="/docs/install">Install</a>.</p>

    <h3>2. Sync once in a terminal</h3>
    <Code code="tether sync" />
    <p>The first sync makes the machine's signing key and signs its record. On macOS, it also moves the daemon to a fixed copy of Tether. See <a href="#macos-app-management">App Management on macOS</a>.</p>
    <p>The sync then asks "Install these packages?". Choose <strong>Decide later</strong>.</p>
    <p>"Install all" approves every package it can name in one step. That includes packages that only unsigned 1.x records list, and formulae from taps you do not trust. Trust your machines first. Their packages then install without a question, and the Inbox keeps only the items that need a look.</p>

    <h3>3. Trust your upgraded machines</h3>
    <p>On each upgraded machine, read its id and full key fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines show" />
    <p>On each other upgraded machine, trust it with the fingerprint you read:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines trust <host> --fingerprint SHA256:..." />
    <p>Read each fingerprint on the machine itself. A fingerprint from the sync repo proves nothing, because anyone who can push can change the repo. If this machine does not have the record yet, <code>machines trust</code> pulls the sync repo first.</p>
    <p>A machine on 1.x has no signed record, so you cannot trust it yet. Upgrade it, sync it, then trust it.</p>

    <h3>4. Sync again and review what is left</h3>
    <Code code={`tether sync
tether packages inbox`} />
    <p>The sync installs the packages that your trusted machines list, when they pass the <a href="/docs/package-security">package checks</a>. The Inbox shows what is left, grouped by source machine and reason. Approve or reject each item, or approve a reviewed group:</p>
    <Code code={`tether packages approve <manager:name>
tether packages approve --all --from <machine>   # after you review the list`} />
    <p><code>approve --all</code> lists the packages and asks first. It leaves machine keys, malicious packages, packages whose record fails its signature, and packages without a version or tap to name in the Inbox. See <a href="/docs/inbox">The Inbox</a>.</p>

    <h3 id="macos-app-management">5. Allow App Management on macOS</h3>
    <p>On macOS, launchd now runs a copy of Tether at <code>~/.tether/bin/tether</code>. The plist names the installed binary in <code>TETHER_DAEMON_SOURCE</code>. When that binary changes, the daemon copies it again and restarts. So the daemon follows each upgrade by itself.</p>
    <DaemonCopyDiagram />
    <p>macOS keeps the App Management permission per program path. The fixed path keeps one permission across upgrades. Before, each upgrade asked again.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The first <code>tether sync</code> in a terminal after the upgrade moves an installed daemon to the copy. <code>tether daemon install</code> does this too</li>
      <li>macOS then asks once for App Management for the new path. Allow it in System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; App Management</li>
      <li>To add the copy by hand in the file picker, press Cmd+Shift+. to show hidden folders such as <code>~/.tether</code></li>
      <li>Remove the old <code>tether</code> rows from the App Management list</li>
      <li><code>tether daemon uninstall</code> removes the copy</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>6. Delete the old plaintext config copy</h3>
    <p>Earlier versions wrote a plaintext copy of the synced config to <code>~/tether/config.toml</code> once, on the first sync of a new machine. Tether no longer writes it. You can delete it:</p>
    <Code code="rm ~/tether/config.toml" />

    <h3>7. Check your settings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Add the third-party Homebrew taps you use to <code>packages.brew.trusted_taps</code>. See <a href="/docs/package-security#homebrew-taps">Homebrew taps</a></li>
      <li>Add packages that need install scripts to <code>packages.allow_scripts</code></li>
      <li>Change <code>packages.min_release_age_days</code> if 7 days does not suit you</li>
      <li>Remove records that <code>tether machines list</code> marks as an old id of this machine. See <a href="/docs/trust#old-machine-ids">Old machine ids</a></li>
      <li>Check that each machine has the profile you want. Packages now go only to the profiles that have them. See <a href="/docs/profiles#package-profiles">Packages per profile</a></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="machines-on-1x">Machines on 1.x</h2>
    <p><code>tether status</code>, <code>tether machines list</code> and the dashboard Machines tab mark each machine that still runs 1.x as "on 1.x".</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A machine on 1.x does not run the new checks. It still installs every synced package without them</li>
      <li>Its record has no signature. Its packages wait in the Inbox of 2.0 machines as "from another machine" until it upgrades and you trust it</li>
      <li>It ignores profiles. It installs the packages of every profile</li>
      <li>A 1.x machine before 1.13.2 pushes its old copy of <code>config.toml</code> again after it applies a synced one. That copy reverts config changes made since, and 2.0 cannot tell it from a real change. Patch or upgrade the 1.x machines, then make the change again</li>
      <li>When a synced config changes this machine's profile, a 2.0 sync warns, names both profiles, and names the command that restores it</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what-changed">What changed in 2.0</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A synced package installs on its own only when a trusted machine record lists it. A line in a manifest grants no trust</li>
      <li>A machine installs only the packages of its own profile. Nothing is uninstalled. The first sync counts the packages that no longer come to this machine, and names the commands that list and share them</li>
      <li>Packages from untrusted Homebrew taps now wait in the Inbox. Before, Tether skipped them with a warning</li>
      <li>For gem, and for npm before 11.10, pnpm before 10.16 and bun before 1.3, Tether checks the release age itself in the registry. It installs the release it checked. It holds a package only when the release is too new or the registry check fails</li>
      <li>Tether checks npm, pnpm, bun, uv and gem packages against OSV before it installs or upgrades them</li>
      <li>npm, pnpm and bun install with install scripts off</li>
      <li>Tether no longer runs <code>brew update</code>. Run it yourself to update Homebrew</li>
      <li><code>tether upgrade</code> asks first, lists what it changes, and never downgrades a package</li>
      <li><code>tether packages</code> now lists packages. <code>tether packages uninstall</code> replaces <code>remove</code>, which stays as an alias</li>
      <li><code>tether ignore secrets</code> and <code>tether ignore files</code> replace the old <code>tether ignore</code> forms, which still work</li>
      <li><code>tether machines rename &lt;NEW&gt;</code> renames only the machine it runs on</li>
      <li><code>-y</code> answers every prompt. It does not approve Inbox items or trust keys. See <a href="/docs/scripting">Exit codes and -y</a></li>
      <li>Every failed command exits with status 1 and prints one <code>Error:</code> line</li>
      <li>A sync merges <code>config.toml</code> three ways, so edits from two machines both stay. See <a href="/docs/config-merge">Config merge</a></li>
      <li>The Config tab no longer shows the <code>sync_versions</code> settings. Tether still writes them as <code>false</code>, because 1.x builds need them to read the synced config</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
